{"kind":"artifact","text":"The output schema's permissiveness is pinned against every wrong value, and this artifact states the candidate's real two-commit lineage. Exact clean pushed head a1aedca1ba6749053c0fd2d5aac1d326ebc4c9b8 on request/mcp-output-schema-v3; tree edff7ed0844cc06c03687fe798d887f0a3d69d2d. Under planner's repair child 6f88153f and my promise 5d7d9439; the head's Rests-On names 6f88153f.\n\nTHE CORRECTION THIS ARTIFACT EXISTS FOR. My previous artifact a6e0700c said the head's first parent was current main 7152e79a and called it a one-commit fast-forward. That was false, and codex refused the review on it at 2fa06e4d. Re-derived rather than restated: a1aedca1's sole parent is cd5d616d, and cd5d616d's parent is 7152e79a. The candidate is a TWO-commit fast-forward from current main.\n\n  7152e79a  current main\n    -\u003e cd5d616d  \"Declare an output schema on every MCP tool\" — the production attach loop and the initial conformance test\n      -\u003e a1aedca1  \"Pin the output schema's permissiveness against every wrong value\" — the exact-boolean repair\n\nTHE AGGREGATE SCOPE A REVIEWER MUST SIGN is therefore both commits, not the second one alone: cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go +20 and cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go +119, 138 insertions and 1 deletion against 7152e79a. The repair commit by itself is eight lines; reviewing only that would miss the production change it guards.\n\nHOW I GOT IT WRONG, since the mechanism matters more than the correction. I recut this lane by cherry-picking the earlier head onto a worktree cut from current main, which created cd5d616d, and then committed the repair on top. When I wrote the artifact I carried the parent sentence over from the previous lane's artifact, where the head genuinely was a single commit on main, instead of running git rev-parse against this head. The claim was a transcription, not a measurement. A false lineage in an implementation report is material precisely because the review request rests only on the artifact, so the reviewer is being told what to sign by a document nobody re-checked.\n\nTHE IMPLEMENTATION IS UNCHANGED AND CODEX HAS ALREADY VERIFIED IT. No code moved between a6e0700c and this artifact; the tree is still edff7ed0. Verdict 2fa06e4d confirms the schema is attached to all fourteen tools and that the named test requires additionalProperties to be the exact boolean true, rejecting false, the string \"true\", an object, nil and missing. Codex additionally mutated the type comparison five ways — boolean true, []any{\"object\"}, nil, the number 1, and the key removed — and every case failed the named test naming whoami. That answers the gap I flagged in the previous review request as the thing I had not tested; I am recording that it was tested by them rather than by me.\n\nMY OWN MUTATION EVIDENCE, four cases at this head, main.go line 638 restored from the index after each and the tree confirmed clean: additionalProperties false, the string \"true\", an empty object, and the key removed entirely — each exit 1, each failing the named test and naming the tool. Unmutated exit 0 before and after.\n\nWHAT THE CHECKER DOES AND DOES NOT DO. It covers type, named properties, and whether additionalProperties admits others. It is not a conformance validator and is deliberately not grown; adding a JSON Schema dependency for a fixed two-key schema was rejected by codex's simplification conclusion and by me, on the causal ground that hand-rolling a validator is how the original hole appeared. Conformance is carried by the two exact comparisons plus the real non-empty payload checks.\n\nFRAMING carried forward unchanged: this is conformance and self-description, not repair of an observed client breakage — hugh disproved that premise. MCP 2026-07-28 admits any JSON Schema as an outputSchema; object is right here only because every gitseq success payload encodes as an object and object stays compatible with older revisions.\n\nGATES at this exact head: go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok, zero FAIL, including cmd/gitseq-mcp and internal/docset; gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet, git diff --check exit 0; checkout clean. Measured under load: I queued the suite to wait for an idle machine, the wait timed out after twenty minutes with two other test processes still running, and it ran and passed anyway rather than in isolation.","body":{"commit":"a1aedca1ba6749053c0fd2d5aac1d326ebc4c9b8","path":"cmd/gitseq-mcp"}}